                        "form": "étantités"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "65002c6a-2018-3edc-8532-52bc2da0cb5c",
                        "entry_id": "7d6c5748-35c6-3e5f-92e1-66c552e1382a",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Ce qui fait qu’un étant est ce qu’il est, sa nature."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "ed513102-eb06-477d-9142-0792abbfa573",
                                "sense_id": "65002c6a-2018-3edc-8532-52bc2da0cb5c",
                                "text": "L'être de l'étant n'est que l'étantité qui comme telle est commune à tous les étants"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "43dcb0f2-93fa-4c25-8483-4de567762500",
                                "sense_id": "65002c6a-2018-3edc-8532-52bc2da0cb5c",
                                "text": "Une première réponse affirmative pourrait être : ce qui fait qu’un étant est un étant, c’est son étance (ou son étantité), οὐσία, Seiendheit, comme on dit: ce qui fait qu’un homme est un homme, c’est son humanité."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "8568f5af-f36b-41f7-8627-8f74ede43ad8",
                                "sense_id": "65002c6a-2018-3edc-8532-52bc2da0cb5c",
                                "text": "Cette compréhension ouvre la voie à un rapprochement avec une autre notion essentielle de la philosophie aristotélicienne, celle d’ousia (littéralement : « l’étance » ou « l’étantité »)"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "b06753a3-7b0c-4e6c-ae7c-8dc39f6b023a",
                                "sense_id": "65002c6a-2018-3edc-8532-52bc2da0cb5c",
                                "text": "L’étantité, la totalité de l’étant, exige la présence de la nature pour atteindre la question de la vérité de l’être"
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "3595817b-ee63-4e3f-a3c8-9d48d8ea2894",
                        "source_entry_id": "7d6c5748-35c6-3e5f-92e1-66c552e1382a",
                        "source_sense_id": "65002c6a-2018-3edc-8532-52bc2da0cb5c",
                        "target_lang_word": "Seiendheit",
                        "target_lang_code": "de",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": []
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "1cbf9fd7-b7c8-490b-b861-cfc6ceef9bbb",
                        "source_entry_id": "7d6c5748-35c6-3e5f-92e1-66c552e1382a",
                        "source_sense_id": "65002c6a-2018-3edc-8532-52bc2da0cb5c",
                        "target_lang_word": "beingness",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "beingness",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "20f46375-5af8-39f0-8896-eb73b1c096c6",
                                        "entry_id": "170c0bfc-9ffa-34dc-aba4-f2b94e79a282",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Existence; the condition of a thing that is."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "uncountable",
                                            "usually"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "f2bf5cee-9846-4522-863d-54e53d0aa524",
                                                "sense_id": "20f46375-5af8-39f0-8896-eb73b1c096c6",
                                                "text": "Thus, from every example, we may see that Quantity always concerns a Beingness, which is indifferent to the very determinateness which it now, or at any time, has."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "e7f47016-12fd-4a0d-b915-fb58bc8a3c80",
                                                "sense_id": "20f46375-5af8-39f0-8896-eb73b1c096c6",
                                                "text": "In the good painting the stone is no longer a stone, i.e., something which we could not possibly be: in the good painting the stone has become visible in its beingness; only in the work of art is the stone truly."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "827b5945-7b4a-4d8c-b176-d25957830e26",
                                                "sense_id": "20f46375-5af8-39f0-8896-eb73b1c096c6",
                                                "text": "The two approaches remain distinct ways of knowing: mystics are interested in the changeless beingness outside of time, while scientists are only interested in understanding the causes of the changes within the world of time."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
